==German==

===Etymology===
{{root|de|ine-pro|*kewk-}}
From {{inh|de|gmh|hōch}}, from {{inh|de|goh|hōh}}, from {{inh|de|gmw-pro|*hauh}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*hauhaz}}, from {{der|de|ine-pro|*kewk-}}, a suffixed form of {{m|ine-pro|*kew-}}. Compare {{cog|nl|hoog}}, {{cog|en|high}}, {{cog|sv|hög}}.

The irregular declension from the stem {{m|de||hoh-}} is due to the development of {{m+|goh||-h-}}, which in Middle High German became {{IPAchar|/x/}} in [[coda]] position, but {{IPAchar|/h/}} elsewhere (which latter was then gradually lost, starting from the north). Stem alternations of this kind were usually levelled in modern German, as for example in {{m|de|nah}}, {{m|de|Schuh}} ({{m+|gmh|nāch}}, {{m|gmh|schuoch}}). Most dialects have also levelled {{m|de||hoch}}, but the standard language has preserved the alternation in this isolated case.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|de|/hoːx/}}
* {{audio|de|De-hoch.ogg|audio (Germany)}}
* {{audio|de|De-at-hoch.ogg|audio (Austria)}}

